## The Problem

Freelancers were paying for tools built for someone else:

- **Tool Overload:** Time, invoices, expenses, taxes and clients lived in different apps that didn't talk to each other.
- **Built for Teams:** Most business software is designed for companies and enterprises, full of features a solo professional never uses.
- **Hidden Mental Cost:** Every switch between apps was a small tax on focus, and small taxes add up fast.

## The Goal

The main objective was to create one calm, focused workspace that handles the business side so freelancers can get back to the actual work.

- **Unify the Workflow:** Bring time tracking, invoicing, expenses, taxes and client management into a single product.
- **Design a Human Experience:** Make admin feel light and approachable instead of corporate and overwhelming.
- **Ship a Real Product:** Build a production-ready web app as a solo designer-developer, using AI as a build partner to move fast without cutting corners.

## My Design & Development Process

### Brand & Naming

TaskBrew began life as *KlaroTrack*. The name worked, but it was cold and didn't say anything about how the product should feel. Renaming it to TaskBrew unlocked the whole identity: a coffee-shop metaphor that became the design system itself. Features got names like *The Tax Roast*, *The Focus Pour*, *The Expense Grind* and *The Clients Lounge*, turning dry admin into something warm and a little playful.

### UX & Interface Design

I designed every screen in Figma around one question: does this make the user feel in control? That meant clear hierarchy, fewer decisions per screen, and dashboards that answer "how am I doing?" at a glance instead of hiding it in reports.

### Technical Challenge: Real-Time Everything

- **The Task:** Timers, totals and tax estimates needed to update instantly across the app, without reloads or lag.
- **The Result:** I built the front-end in React, Vite and Tailwind, with Firebase handling authentication, real-time data and storage, so every number updates the moment something changes.

## Final Results & Reflection

TaskBrew is the project where I stopped being only the designer and became the whole product team. It's currently in **beta** at taskbrew.app, used by me and a group of freelance designer friends, and using it every day to run my own studio is honestly the best (and most brutal) user testing there is.

- **Designing for Myself, Honestly:** Being my own user forced me to cut every feature that didn't earn its place. Simplicity turned out to be the hardest thing to design.
- **Naming Is Design:** The moment KlaroTrack became TaskBrew, the product stopped being a list of features and got a personality. Every design decision after that got easier.
- **Full-Stack Confidence:** Taking a product from Figma to a live app with auth, a database and real users proved I can own the whole journey, not just the screens.

## What's Brewing Next

TaskBrew is in beta, used daily by me and a small group of freelance designers. The product is built and stable; the next chapter is about growing it beyond that first circle.

- **Beta Feedback:** Learning from how fellow designers use it day to day to refine the core flows and prioritize what gets built next.
- **Expanding the Reach:** Planning to open TaskBrew to more freelancers beyond the design community.
- **Exploring a Business Model:** Evaluating whether TaskBrew grows into a paid product, and if so, what pricing would feel fair to the solo professionals it was made for.
